  "notes": "1927 four-level brick flats (garage storey + 3 storeys of flats; the roll's 3 leaves the garage storey out), S side of Chestnut Street between Webster and Fillmore. Red-brown running-bond face brick; three cream three-sided bays over the upper storeys (one over each ground opening group); plain brick between; white double-hung sashes; cream string course over the ground storey and a bracketed cream cornice; steel fire escape on the west part. Ground: blue side doors at both ends, two red-panel sectional garage doors in white frame grids, recessed central entry with an ornamented cream surround and dark iron gate. Signage: house number only. Garage panels and side-door leaves on a white kit base (the kit tint multiplies, so steel went black); closed royal-blue panelled side doors; recessed dark entry vestibule under a shouldered head with cream pilasters, gilt capitals and keystone, iron gate folded open; large white double-hung bay sashes with divided upper sashes; red glazed-tile spandrel plaques with gilt relief on every storey; thin continuous string course round the bay feet; platform-and-ladder fire escape hung on the west bay; cream stucco rear and a tan clapboard east party return; ground storey 3.0 m. Clean white-painted bays with shallower angled sides so every face is nearly all sash (wide multi-lite uppers over one-light lowers); sculpted spandrel tile panels (sunk red field, gilt border, relief figure and volutes) filling each spandrel; a deep white dentilled cornice across the whole front with the bays rising into its frieze; a light-painted single-balcony fire escape with a roof ladder and braces; a white plinth, white garage casings to the pavement, chunky white rails on both faces; a projecting white belt course; deep-blue panelled side doors, the west one with a glazed grille light; the entrance as a dark recessed vestibule with a closed iron gate at the back under a flat shouldered head in a polychrome and gilt surround; interior room doors and no corridor sconces. Thinner garage rails (red panels dominate), two-row upper sashes. Dark reflective bay glass (tinted clear glass over dark room tones, blinds and curtains drawn at mixed heights), sashes set back in a reveal with mixed 1-over-1 and 6-over-1 patterns; spandrel tiles as large gilt twin-dragon scroll reliefs round a blue and red medallion with dark shading; a thicker, arched entrance surround (wide gilt-ornamented pilasters, a deep frieze, a stepped white architrave) with no centre gate post and a dark stone floor; duller darker brick; a modillioned cornice; 7-row garage doors a little narrower and lower on white stepped sills; a taller garage storey (3.4 m); a dark steel fire escape ending at the roof line; frames instead of corridor sconces. Fire escape moved onto the brick pier between the centre and end bays; deeper crisp cream ledge; glazed grille light on both blue side doors.",
